- How would you adapt suggest to support prefix similarity (typo-tolerant search)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DP with a multi‑dimensional state and non‑trivial transitions, mixing path constraints with exact move counts. It also probes memoization vs bottom‑up, boundary handling, and memory optimizations essential for large grids.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) representing position and moves
- Transitions from (i-1,j,t-1), (i,j-1,t-1), (i-1,j-1,t-1)
- -INF for invalid states; base cases
- Memory optimization via rolling arrays
- Trade-offs between readability (memoized DFS) and performance (tabulation)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridSum(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array.from({length:m}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  function dfs(i,j,t){
    if(i<0||j<0||t<0) return NEG;
    if(i===0&&j===0&&t===0) return grid[0][0];
    if(memo[i][j][t]!==NEG) return memo[i][j][t];
    const best = Math.max(dfs(i-1,j,t-1), dfs(i,j-1,t-1), dfs(i-1,j-1,t-1));
    memo[i][j][t] = (best===NEG)? NEG : grid[i][j] + best;
    return memo[i][j][t];
  }
  return dfs(n-1,m-1,k);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the path achieving the maximum sum?
- How would this change if some moves were disallowed or weights negative?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to implement non-recursive mergesort on a linked list, a common real-world pattern where recursion is costly or prohibited. Tests pointer manipulation, stability of merge, and correctness of bottom-up passes, plus O(n log n) time and O(1) extra space.

## Key Concepts

- Bottom-up mergesort
- In-place linked-list merge
- Stability
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function mergeTwo(l1, l2) {
  const dummy = { val: 0, next: null };
  let tail = dummy;
  while (l1 && l2) {
    if (l1.val <= l2.val) {
      tail.next = l1;
      l1 = l1.next;
    } else {
      tail.next = l2;
      l2 = l2.next;
    }
    tail = tail.next;
  }
  tail.next = l1 || l2;
  return dummy.next;
}

function sortList(head) {
  if (!head || !head.next) return head;
  // length
  let len = 0, p = head;
  while (p) { len++; p = p.next; }
  const dummy = { next: head };
  for (let w = 1; w < len; w <<= 1) {
    let prev = dummy;
    let curr = dummy.next;
    while (curr) {
      // split left
      let left = curr;
      let i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const right = curr.next;
      curr.next = null;
      // split right
      curr = right;
      i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const next = curr ? curr.next : null;
      if (curr) curr.next = null;
      const merged = mergeTwo(left, right);
      prev.next = merged;
      while (prev.next) prev = prev.next;
      curr = next;
    }
  }
  return dummy.next;
}
```

